    <help><![CDATA[
Evaluate ChIP-seq reproducibility for two biological replicates
================================================================

Provide two ChIP BAM files and their matched control BAM files. Choose BAMPE
for paired-end data and BAM for single-end data. All files must refer to the
same reference genome. In the accompanying PP script, pooled BAMs are
concatenated after sequence-dictionary checks; coordinate sorting is not
required. Pseudoreplicate assignment keeps the mates of each template together
and produces approximately equal complementary halves.

The tool executes:

* **TR:** IDR between the two biological replicates (Nt).
* **PP:** IDR between the two pooled pseudoreplicates (Np).
* **SP:** IDR between the two self pseudoreplicates of each biological replicate
  (N1 and N2).

Existing narrowPeak files can replace peak calling in TR only. They must be in
replicate order and have been generated using the same peak-calling parameters
and preprocessing strategy as those selected here. BAM files remain required
for PP and SP.

The summary reports rescue_ratio = max(Nt, Np) / min(Nt, Np) and
self_consistency_ratio = max(N1, N2) / min(N1, N2). A ratio is reported as NA
when either corresponding count is zero. Counts include only peaks passing
the selected global IDR threshold.

IDR peak outputs use the tabular datatype because IDR adds columns to the
standard narrowPeak fields. MACS2 narrowPeak files use the BED datatype in
Galaxy. The narrowPeak collection contains the MACS2 peaks
generated by this run: eight files in BAM-only mode, six when existing peaks
are supplied. The supplied TR peaks remain in the history and are not copied
into this collection.

Enable **Keep pseudoreplicate BAM files** to retain the exact treatment and
control BAM halves used for PP and SP peak calling. The optional BAM collection
contains twelve files (four pooled, four for replicate 1, four for replicate 2).
They are written in output/pseudo_replicate_bam/ and kept independently of
temporary-directory cleanup. This option is disabled by default. BAMs are
returned using the unsorted.bam datatype because the pooled BAMs are not
coordinate sorted. No additional sorting or indexing is performed.

The branch manifest contains paths inside the job working directory; use the
separate Galaxy output datasets to access those results after the job ends.
The internal sample name is fixed to idr and replicate labels to rep1/rep2.
Output labels identify the Galaxy input datasets.

CPU allocation is obtained from GALAXY_SLOTS. MACS2 calls and branches currently
run sequentially; the thread allocation applies to samtools.
